 \begin{abstract}
-Thermodynamic speed limits relate finite-time state change to physical quantities such as entropy production and calibrated dynamical activity. We isolate a smaller audit question: what follows when an operator reports a gross discrete activity budget but explicitly reserves part of that activity for non-progress work? For nonnegative integers, a supplied progress bound $d\le n u$ and the decomposition $g=u+r$ imply $d+n r\le n g$. The result is addition monotonicity and distributivity, so the triviality probe classifies it as a methods note. Exhaustive checks and a frozen Lean candidate test only this accounting statement. They do not derive a stochastic-thermodynamic speed limit, calibrate physical activity, or validate an ecological application.
